  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer and more humid today with isolated afternoon\nthunderstorms possible.\n\nLow level moisture has increased over the area early this\nmorning as evidence of low clouds on satellite imagery and sfc \ndewpoints now in the mid 60s compared to the mid 50s yesterday.\nHigh pressure building to the south of the area today will \nallow for a warmer and more unstable air mass. A developing \nsurface trof may be able to spark a few thunderstorms this \nafternoon in an increasingly hot, humid, and unstable air mass.\nThunderstorms should dissipate quickly as we approach sunset.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Late summer heat returns this week.\n\nGuidance remains steadfast on late summer heat returning to end \nmeteorological summer (June 1-August 31) and begin meteorological \nautumn (September 1-November 30).\n\nA strong mid-level ridge will build across the southern CONUS and \ninto the Mid-Atlantic, then persist through at least the middle of \nthe week. Some guidance has been trending towards having the ridge \naxis a bit further south and west, which would lower heights a bit \nand introduce more chances for showers and thunderstorms at times in \nlarge scale NW flow aloft. This lends to a bit of spread in the \ntemperature forecast, especially if any back-door surface cold \nfronts slide into the region by mid/late week.\n\nDespite the uncertainties, it remains likely that high temperatures \nwill reach the lower to middle 90s for much of the region for much \nof the new work week. Latest guidance indicates potential for \ntemperatures in the upper 90s to near 100 mid week, so long as \nit is not disrupted by cloud cover or convection. Depending on \nsmaller scale details, dew points could end up a little higher \nor lower than currently progged which will ultimately affect how\nhigh heat index values get. Values approaching Heat Advisory \ncriteria (100-104 west of the Blue Ridge, 105-109 east of the \nBlue Ridge) are possible.\n\nTemperatures may begin to moderate by next weekend as a cold front \ncrosses the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Thunderstorm chances increase this week.\n\nDisturbances rounding a ridge to the south and west in NW flow aloft \nwill introduce shower and thunderstorm chances at times this \nweek. The timing and amplitude of these waves have very low \npredictability beyond a couple days in advance (at best). But \nthe general theme will be enhanced lift and shear clashing with \nan increasingly hot and unstable airmass over or near the Mid- \nAtlantic. AI/ML guidance has broad elevated probabilities of \nsevere weather much of this week, particularly Tuesday through \nThursday (and possibly Friday). Guidance is currently most keen \non a shortwave impulse passing just to the north on Wednesday.\n\nIt should be noted that there is a conditional potential for one or \nmore organized clusters of strong to severe storms at some point \nthis week. The most likely time currently appears to be around \nmid week, but changes are to be expected at this time range.\n\nStorm chances may linger into next weekend as a cold front\ncrosses.",
